This paper consolidates three bodies of work into one account. Each result carries its source and its status: proved, computed, measured, open, or closed by theorem.
1. Hierarchy recovery [H]. A gradient-free estimator (spectral initialisation, soft hand-off, inside–outside EM and split–merge) recovers a depth-4 probabilistic grammar to 0.001 nats per sequence of the true model in 131 s. Twelve transformer configurations never represent the level-3 latent symbol.
2. Cohomological strand [O]. Holonomy of known operations gives a genuine obstruction [g] ∈ H¹(Γ, A). Marginal obstructions within one data source vanish (T0). The information site and the contextuality site are separated by a theorem.
3. Results of the present work [S]:
 an exact identifiability criterion for holonomy estimated from data, with a calibrated test and its sample cost;
 a direct construction from operations to outcomes, [g]̸ = 0 ⇐⇒ γ̸ = 0, which is invisible to the probability layer;
 for lossy operations: a closed form CF = max(0, 1−|L|ε/2) on a cycle, and a holonomy-girth lower bound on general graphs;
 the loop spectrum as a monoid-valued holonomy invariant;
 a decorated Bregman– ˇCech nerve that carries orientation and phase, with a codimension criterion for when it can;
 restriction and extension maps between the information and contextuality views;
 a gated contextuality test for streams with not-applicable entries, with a pinned maximum-likelihood null.
The organising statement is that the three layers (operations, probability and outcomes) respond to the same data through different invariants, and that no invariant of one layer determines another.
